---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Is Chinese Street Food Safe? Complete Safety Guide | ChineseSnacks" description="A practical guide to eating street food safely in China. What to look for, what to avoid, and how to handle common issues. Updated for 2026.">
  <Nav />
  <div class="min-h-[100dvh] pt-24 pb-20 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto w-full pt-16">
      <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-wide uppercase text-accent mb-3">Safety Guide</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-black tracking-tighter mb-6">Is Chinese Street Food Safe?</h1>
      <div class="max-w-[65ch] space-y-8 text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-lg">Yes, Chinese street food is generally safe when you follow basic rules. Millions of people eat it every day. But there are real things to watch for, and this guide covers them honestly.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">The Golden Rules</h2>
        <p>The single most reliable safety signal in any country: <strong class="text-ink">eat where locals eat.</strong> Long queues and high turnover mean food isn't sitting around. If a stall is empty during peak hours, there's a reason.</p>
        <p>Cooked-to-order is always safer than pre-cooked food sitting in a warming tray. Watch the cooking process — if oil looks dark and murky, move on. Clean oil is pale gold.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">How to Spot a Clean Stall</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-100 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-green-800 mb-2 text-sm">Green Flags</h3>
            <ul class="text-sm text-green-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>Vendor wears gloves or uses tongs/chopsticks to handle food</li>
              <li>High customer turnover — trays empty and refill fast</li>
              <li>Separate cutting boards for raw and cooked food</li>
              <li>Ingredients stored in covered containers or a refrigerated display</li>
              <li>Clean work surface, no visible grease buildup</li>
              <li>Fresh oil — light gold, not dark brown or smoking</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2 text-sm">Red Flags</h3>
            <ul class="text-sm text-red-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>Raw meat sitting at room temperature for extended periods</li>
              <li>Flies or insects around the food display</li>
              <li>Reusing cooking oil until it's black and smoking</li>
              <li>Empty stall during peak meal hours</li>
              <li>Vendor handling money and food with the same hands</li>
              <li>Pre-cooked dishes that look dry or have been sitting for hours</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">Water & Drinks</h2>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Do not drink tap water in China.</strong> This includes brushing your teeth with tap water — use bottled water. Ice in drinks is generally safe in restaurants and modern cafes, but skip ice from street vendors unless you see them using commercially packaged ice.</p>
        <p>Bottled water is cheap and everywhere. Nongfu Spring (农夫山泉) and C'estbon (怡宝) are the major brands. In restaurants, hot tea has been boiled and is always safe.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">Common Ailments & What to Do</h2>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Traveler's diarrhea</strong> is the most common issue. It usually passes within 24-48 hours. Stay hydrated — oral rehydration salts (ORS) are available at any Chinese pharmacy. Ask for <em>kǒu fú bǔ yè yán</em> (口服补液盐).</p>
        <p>Carry Imodium (loperamide) for emergencies, but use it sparingly — it's better to let mild cases run their course. Activated charcoal tablets can help with mild food reactions.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">Regional Differences</h2>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          <p><strong class="text-ink">Beijing:</strong> Generally high standards. Wangfujing's tourist stalls are visually shocking (scorpions on sticks) but the actual food safety is fine — these stalls are heavily regulated.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-ink">Chengdu & Chongqing:</strong> The spicy mala broth in hot pot and chuan chuan is boiling hot, which kills most bacteria. Sichuan food's heavy use of chili oil also has mild antimicrobial properties. Still, check that your chuan chuan ingredients are refrigerated.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-ink">Xi'an:</strong> The Muslim Quarter's lamb skewers are grilled at high heat over charcoal — generally safe. Avoid raw vegetable garnishes.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-ink">Shanghai:</strong> Higher hygiene standards overall. Street stalls here tend to be more permanent and regulated than in other cities.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">Medical Help</h2>
        <p>If symptoms last more than 48 hours, involve a fever, or include blood — see a doctor. International clinics in major cities include:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
          <li><strong>Beijing:</strong> Beijing United Family Hospital (和睦家), Chaoyang District</li>
          <li><strong>Shanghai:</strong> Shanghai United Family Hospital, Changning District</li>
          <li><strong>Chengdu:</strong> West China Hospital (华西医院) — China's top-rated hospital</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Travel insurance with medical coverage is strongly recommended. Many international clinics require upfront payment.</p>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-8">Bottom Line</h2>
        <p>Chinese street food is one of the world's great culinary experiences. The risk is real but manageable. Eat where it's busy, trust your eyes, avoid raw food from street stalls, and you'll almost certainly have an amazing time — and zero regrets.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
